Given a doubly linked list of n elements. Return the doubly linked list as an array.

Note: The driver code will print the list forward and backward.

Examples:

Input: LinkedList: 1<->2<->3<->4<->5Output:1 2 3 4 5

5 4 3 2 1

Explanation: After traversing forward the linked list will look like 1 2 3 4 5 and from backward will 5 4 3 2 1.

Input: LinkedList: 34<->22Output:34 22

22 34

Explanation: After traversing forward the linked list will look like 34 22 and from backward will 22 34.

Input: LinkedList: 1Output:1

1

Constraints:

1 ≤ n ≤ 103

0 ≤ value of nodes ≤ 103

Here is the straightforward and optimal solution to convert a doubly linked list into an array.

The Logic
To extract the elements into an array, you only need to traverse the doubly linked list in the forward direction. Since the driver code handles the backward printing, your only responsibility is to populate and return the sequence.

Initialize an empty dynamic array (like a vector in C++, ArrayList in Java, or list in Python).

Create a current pointer and set it to the head of the linked list.

Use a while loop that runs as long as current is not null.

In each iteration, push current.data into your array.

Move the pointer forward using current = current.next.

Return the array.

Code Implementations
Python 3

Python
class Solution:
    def constructList(self, head):
        result = []
        curr = head
        
        while curr is not None:
            result.append(curr.data)
            curr = curr.next
            
        return result
Java

Java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Integer> constructList(Node head) {
        ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Node curr = head;
        
        while (curr != null) {
            result.add(curr.data);
            curr = curr.next;
        }
        
        return result;
    }
}
C++

C++
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> constructList(Node *head) {
        vector<int> result;
        Node* curr = head;
        
        while (curr != nullptr) {
            result.push_back(curr->data);
            curr = curr->next;
        }
        
        return result;
    }
};
Complexity Analysis
Time Complexity: O(n) where n is the number of nodes in the doubly linked list. We visit each node exactly once.

Space Complexity: O(n) to store the elements in the newly created array that will be returned. No extra auxiliary space is used.

